"""Sales & CRM toolkit: lead follow-ups, pipeline forecast, targets, customers, price list, visit reports and an online quotation maker.
   Global toolkit: every workbook has a currency symbol setting and uses unambiguous dates (05-Nov-2026); the online tool picks
   currency, number style and date format."""

KITS = [
    dict(id='sales', name='Sales & CRM', icon='📈', color='#E11D48',
         title='Sales & CRM Toolkit: Lead Tracker, Sales Pipeline and Quotation Templates',
         desc='Free Excel lead follow-up tracker, sales pipeline forecast, target vs achievement sheet, customer list and price list, Word visit reports and an online quotation maker.',
         intro=['Most small sales teams do not lose deals because the product is wrong. They lose them because a follow-up call slipped, a quotation went out late, or nobody noticed that a good customer had stopped ordering three months ago.',
                'This toolkit is a simple CRM you already know how to use: Excel workbooks for leads, the pipeline, monthly targets, customers and prices, Word formats for field visit reports, and an online quotation and proforma invoice maker. Each workbook has a currency setting, so it works in rupees, dollars, dirhams or any other currency, and your data stays on your own computer.'],
         who='Salespeople, sales managers, business owners who sell themselves, field sales and dealer networks, B2B suppliers, agencies and service businesses anywhere in the world.'),
]

ITEMS = [
    dict(id='sales-lead-tracker-excel', kit='sales', kind='excel', icon='📇', name='Lead & Follow-up Tracker (Excel)',
         title='Lead Tracker in Excel: Free Sales Follow-up Sheet with Overdue Alerts',
         desc='Free Excel lead tracker: source, stage, owner, deal value and next follow-up date for every enquiry. Overdue and due-today follow-ups light up; a summary shows win rate by source.',
         files=[('Sales-Lead-Follow-up-Tracker.xlsx', 'Lead and follow-up tracker workbook')],
         preview={'Sales-Lead-Follow-up-Tracker.xlsx': ('Leads', 'A3:P10')},
         lead='Every enquiry becomes one row: who it is, where it came from, what they want, roughly what it is worth, who owns it and when you will call next. The workbook flags follow-ups that are overdue, due today or due in the next few days, so nothing waits in someone\'s memory. A Summary sheet counts leads by stage, shows which sources actually turn into orders, and lists each salesperson\'s open, overdue and won leads.',
         inside=['Leads sheet with 500 ready rows: date added, company, contact person, phone, email, city, source, product of interest, estimated value, owner, stage, last contact, next follow-up date and next action',
                 'Follow-up status that fills itself: Overdue (red), Today (amber), Soon, Upcoming, No date set or Closed, plus the number of days to go or days late',
                 'Lead age in days, so old enquiries that never moved are easy to spot',
                 'Setup sheet with your currency symbol, the "soon" warning window and editable lists of salespeople, lead sources, stages and products',
                 'Summary sheet: open and overdue counts, open pipeline value, win rate, leads and won value by source, by stage and by salesperson, and a chart of leads by source'],
         steps=['Open the Setup sheet. Type your currency symbol (₹, $, €, £, AED or any other) and how many days ahead a follow-up should show as "Soon".',
                'Still on Setup, replace the sample salespeople, lead sources and products with your own. Keep the stage names unless you have a reason to change them; Won and Lost must stay as they are because the formulas look for them.',
                'Go to the Leads sheet and delete the sample rows (select the yellow cells from row 4 down and press Delete).',
                'For every new enquiry add a row: date, company or person, contact details, source, what they want, a rough value and the owner. Set the stage to New.',
                'After each call or meeting, type the date in Last contact, choose the new stage, write the next action and set the Next follow-up date. Never leave an open lead without a next date.',
                'Start each day with Data › Filter on the Follow-up status column: work the Overdue and Today rows first.',
                'When a deal closes, set the stage to Won or Lost. Closed leads stop showing alerts but still count in the Summary.'],
         tips=['Write the next action as a verb and an object, for example "Send revised quote" or "Call after budget meeting", so anyone can pick it up.',
               'Record the real source of every lead. After two or three months the Summary shows which sources bring orders and which only bring calls.',
               'A lead that has been at the same stage for weeks is usually lost. Ask the customer directly, then mark it Won or Lost so your pipeline stays honest.',
               'Dates show as 05-Nov-2026 so they read the same in every country. To change the look, select the column and use Home › Number Format › More Number Formats.',
               'Share the file through OneDrive, Google Drive or SharePoint if several salespeople update it; ask each person to edit only their own rows.'],
         faqs=[('Is this a CRM?', 'It covers the core of one: a lead list, stages, owners, follow-up reminders and reports. It does not send emails or messages and it has no app; for a team of one to ten people it is often all that is needed before paying for CRM software.'),
               ('When does a follow-up show as overdue?', 'When the Next follow-up date is before today and the stage is not Won or Lost. On the day itself it shows as Today, and within the number of days you set on the Setup sheet it shows as Soon.'),
               ('How is the win rate worked out?', 'Won leads divided by all closed leads (Won plus Lost). Open leads are left out, because they have not been decided yet.'),
               ('Can I add my own stages?', 'Yes. Add or rename stages in the Stages list on the Setup sheet and they appear in the drop-down. Keep Won and Lost spelt exactly like that, because the alerts and the win rate depend on them.'),
               ('Does it work in Google Sheets?', 'Yes. Upload it to Google Drive and open it with Google Sheets: the formulas, drop-down lists and colour highlights all work.')]),

    dict(id='sales-pipeline-forecast-excel', kit='sales', kind='excel', icon='🔻', name='Sales Pipeline & Forecast (Excel)',
         title='Sales Pipeline Template in Excel: Weighted Forecast by Stage and Month',
         desc='Free Excel sales pipeline: deals with value, stage and expected close date; each stage has a win probability, giving a weighted forecast by stage, month and salesperson with charts.',
         files=[('Sales-Pipeline-Forecast.xlsx', 'Sales pipeline and forecast workbook')],
         preview={'Sales-Pipeline-Forecast.xlsx': ('Forecast', 'A3:E14')},
         lead='A pipeline answers the question every owner asks on Monday morning: how much business is likely to close, and when? List your open deals with their value, stage and expected close date. Each stage carries a win probability, so a large deal at the first meeting counts for much less than a smaller one in final negotiation. The Forecast sheet adds it all up by stage, by month for the next six months and by salesperson, with two charts.',
         inside=['Setup sheet: currency symbol, forecast start month, salespeople and a stage table with editable win probabilities (Prospecting 10%, Qualified 25%, Proposal sent 50%, Negotiation 75%, Commit 90%, Won 100%, Lost 0%)',
                 'Deals sheet with 500 rows: deal, customer, owner, value, stage, created date, expected close date, next step and next step date; probability, weighted value and warnings fill themselves',
                 'Warnings for open deals whose close date has passed and for deals with no next step date',
                 'Forecast sheet: deals, value and weighted value by stage; a six-month forecast with pipeline value, weighted value and won value; a table by salesperson',
                 'Charts: pipeline value by stage and weighted forecast by month'],
         steps=['On the Setup sheet type your currency symbol and the first month you want in the forecast (any date in that month).',
                'Check the win probability for each stage. If your team closes half the deals that reach Negotiation, type 50% there instead of 75%.',
                'Replace the sample salespeople with your team.',
                'On the Deals sheet delete the sample rows and enter your open deals: deal name, customer, owner, value, stage and the date you honestly expect it to close.',
                'Update the stage, close date and next step every week. Mark deals Won or Lost as soon as they are decided.',
                'Open the Forecast sheet for the totals and charts. Use the weighted figure for planning and the full pipeline value as the best case.'],
         tips=['A forecast is only as good as its close dates. When a customer delays, move the date instead of leaving it in the past; the sheet marks late dates in red to remind you.',
               'After a few months compare your weighted forecast with what actually closed and adjust the stage probabilities to match your team\'s real results.',
               'Keep one deal per row even for repeat customers, so each order has its own stage and date.',
               'Won deals stay in the sheet and show in the Won column of the monthly forecast, which gives you a simple record of what was booked each month.'],
         faqs=[('What is a weighted forecast?', 'Each deal\'s value multiplied by the win probability of its stage, then added up. A deal worth 100,000 at Proposal sent (50%) adds 50,000. Across many deals this gives a more realistic figure than adding up full values.'),
               ('Why use stages instead of a gut-feel percentage for each deal?', 'Stage-based probabilities are consistent: everyone on the team means the same thing by "Negotiation". If a particular deal is unusual, you can still note it in the Next step column.'),
               ('Which deals count in the forecast?', 'Open deals (every stage except Won and Lost) count in the pipeline and weighted columns, placed in the month of their expected close date. Won deals are shown separately. Lost deals are ignored.'),
               ('Can I see a forecast for more than six months?', 'Change the forecast start month on the Setup sheet to move the six-month window forward or back.')]),

    dict(id='sales-target-achievement-excel', kit='sales', kind='excel', icon='🎯', name='Sales Target vs Achievement (Excel)',
         title='Sales Target vs Achievement in Excel: Monthly Salesperson Scorecard',
         desc='Free Excel sales target tracker: monthly targets per salesperson, a sales entry sheet, month and year-to-date achievement percent, gap, rank and a target vs achieved chart.',
         files=[('Sales-Target-vs-Achievement.xlsx', 'Sales target vs achievement workbook')],
         preview={'Sales-Target-vs-Achievement.xlsx': ('Scorecard', 'A5:K12')},
         lead='Set a target for every salesperson for each month of the year, record sales as they happen, and the scorecard shows who is ahead and who needs help: this month\'s target and sales, year-to-date target and sales, achievement percent, the gap still to cover and a rank. Choose any report month and the whole scorecard moves to that month.',
         inside=['Setup sheet: company name, currency symbol, the first month of your sales year (April, January or any other) and the report month',
                 'Targets sheet: 12 monthly targets for up to 30 salespeople, with region and yearly total',
                 'Sales sheet with 2,000 rows: date, salesperson, customer, invoice or order number, product and amount',
                 'Achievement sheet: actual sales per salesperson per month, calculated from the Sales sheet',
                 'Scorecard: month target, month sales and percent; year-to-date target, sales, percent and gap; rank and a status (Achieved, Close, Behind) in colour, plus a team total',
                 'Chart of year-to-date target against sales for each salesperson'],
         steps=['On the Setup sheet type your company name and currency symbol, then the first day of your sales year (for example 01-Apr-2026 for an April to March year, or 01-Jan-2026).',
                'On the Targets sheet replace the sample names and type each person\'s target for every month. Months that need no target can be 0.',
                'Record each sale on the Sales sheet: date, salesperson (from the list), customer, bill number, product and amount before tax. You can also paste a month\'s sales from your billing software.',
                'Type the report month on the Setup sheet (any date in that month). The Scorecard shows that month and the year to date up to it.',
                'Print the Scorecard or save it as PDF for the monthly sales meeting.'],
         tips=['Decide once whether targets and sales are counted before or after tax and returns, and stick to it; mixing them makes the percentages meaningless.',
               'Enter credit notes and returns as negative amounts on the Sales sheet so achievement reflects real sales.',
               'Ranks use the year-to-date percent, not the amount, so a new salesperson with a smaller target can still rank first.',
               'For a team target, add a row named "Team" on the Targets sheet only if you also record its sales under that name; the Scorecard already adds every person into a team total.'],
         faqs=[('How is achievement percent calculated?', 'Sales divided by target for the same period. If the target is 0 the percent is left blank instead of showing an error.'),
               ('What do Achieved, Close and Behind mean?', 'Achieved is 100 percent or more of the year-to-date target, Close is 80 to 99 percent, and Behind is below 80 percent. Change the 80 percent level on the Setup sheet if your company uses another rule.'),
               ('My year starts in April. Will the months line up?', 'Yes. Whatever first month you type on the Setup sheet becomes month 1 and the 12 target columns follow from it.'),
               ('Can I use it for collections or for product targets?', 'Yes. Rename "Salesperson" to a product or region and record collections or product sales instead; the formulas work the same way.')]),

    dict(id='sales-customer-list-excel', kit='sales', kind='excel', icon='👥', name='Customer List with Last Purchase (Excel)',
         title='Customer List Template in Excel: Last Purchase, Total Sales and Lapsed Customers',
         desc='Free Excel customer database: contact details, segment and owner, with first and last purchase date, order count, total and average sales, and Active, At risk or Lapsed status.',
         files=[('Sales-Customer-List.xlsx', 'Customer list workbook')],
         preview={'Sales-Customer-List.xlsx': ('Customers', 'A3:O9')},
         lead='Your existing customers are the cheapest sales you will ever make, if you notice when they go quiet. Keep every customer\'s details on one sheet and record purchases on another; the list then shows when each customer first and last bought, how many orders they placed, their total and average order, and whether they are Active, At risk or Lapsed. A summary lists your top ten customers and sales by segment.',
         inside=['Customers sheet for 300 customers: ID, name, contact person, phone, email, city or region, segment, account owner and notes',
                 'Calculated columns: customer since, last purchase, days since last purchase, number of orders, total sales, average order value and status',
                 'Purchases sheet with 2,000 rows: date, customer (drop-down), invoice or order number, product and amount',
                 'Setup sheet: currency symbol, the Active and At risk limits in days (90 and 180 to start) and the segment list',
                 'Summary sheet: customers by status, sales by segment and the top ten customers by total sales'],
         steps=['On the Setup sheet type your currency symbol and check the day limits: a customer who bought within 90 days is Active, within 180 days At risk, and older than that Lapsed. Change them to suit how often your customers usually buy.',
                'On the Customers sheet replace the samples with your customers. Give each a short ID and a unique name, because purchases are matched by name.',
                'Record purchases on the Purchases sheet: date, customer from the drop-down list, bill number, product and amount. You can paste them from your billing software every month.',
                'Sort or filter the Customers sheet by Status to see who has lapsed, or by Days since last purchase to see who to call this week.',
                'Open the Summary sheet for your top customers and sales by segment.'],
         tips=['Call At risk customers before they become Lapsed: a short "anything you need this month?" call wins back more orders than a discount later.',
               'Use the Segment column for the way you sell (retail, wholesale, corporate, online, distributor) so the Summary shows which kind of customer brings the most money.',
               'If a customer changes name, change it on both sheets (Home › Find & Select › Replace) so their history stays together.',
               'The amounts are whatever you choose to record: with or without tax. Use the same rule every time.'],
         faqs=[('How does it know the last purchase date?', 'It finds the latest date on the Purchases sheet for that customer\'s name. If there is no purchase yet, the status says "No purchase yet".'),
               ('What is a lapsed customer?', 'One who has not bought for longer than your At risk limit (180 days to start). The right limit depends on your business: a grocery wholesaler may treat 30 days as lapsed, a machinery supplier two years.'),
               ('Can I keep several years of purchases?', 'Yes, up to 2,000 purchase rows in this file. If you need more, insert rows above the last row of the Purchases sheet and the formulas include them.'),
               ('Is my customer data safe?', 'The file stays on your computer or wherever you save it. Treat it as confidential: it holds names and phone numbers, so do not share it publicly.')]),

    dict(id='sales-price-list-excel', kit='sales', kind='excel', icon='🏷️', name='Price List with Dealer Prices (Excel)',
         title='Price List Template in Excel: List, Dealer and Distributor Prices with Margin',
         desc='Free Excel price list: products with cost, list price and margin, automatic dealer and distributor prices, tax-inclusive price, and a printable customer price list in any currency.',
         files=[('Sales-Price-List.xlsx', 'Price list workbook')],
         preview={'Sales-Price-List.xlsx': ('Price List', 'A1:H10')},
         lead='Keep one master list of products with their cost and list price, and let the workbook work out the margin, the dealer and distributor prices from your discount rates, and the price including tax. A separate Price List sheet prints a clean, customer-facing list for the price level you choose, without showing your cost or margin, and leaves out discontinued products.',
         inside=['Setup sheet: company details, currency symbol, effective date, tax name (GST, VAT or sales tax), dealer and distributor discounts, price rounding and a low-margin warning level',
                 'Products sheet for 400 products: code, name, category, unit, pack size, cost price, list price, tax rate, minimum order quantity, status and notes',
                 'Calculated margin percent with a red warning below your minimum, dealer price, distributor price and list price including tax',
                 'Price List sheet ready for A4 printing or PDF: pick List, Dealer or Distributor and it shows those prices with tax, for active products only, with your company header'],
         steps=['On the Setup sheet type your company name, address, contact details, currency symbol, the tax name used in your country and the date the prices take effect.',
                'Type the dealer and distributor discounts off the list price (for example 15% and 25%) and how prices should be rounded (to 1, 5, 10 or 0 for no rounding).',
                'On the Products sheet replace the samples with your products: code, name, category, unit, pack size, cost price, list price and tax rate.',
                'Check the Margin column. Anything below your minimum margin turns red: raise the price or review the cost.',
                'Mark products you no longer sell as Discontinued. They stay on the Products sheet but disappear from the printed list.',
                'Open the Price List sheet, pick the price level in the yellow cell and print it or save it as PDF to send to customers.'],
         tips=['Margin here is profit as a percent of the selling price ((list price minus cost) divided by list price). Markup on cost is a different, larger number; do not mix the two.',
               'Send dealers the Dealer version and retail customers the List version from the same file, so the two lists can never disagree.',
               'Change the effective date every time you revise prices, and keep a PDF of each old list for reference.',
               'Tax rates differ by country and product. Check the correct rate for each product with your accountant and type it in the Tax % column.'],
         faqs=[('How are dealer and distributor prices worked out?', 'List price minus the discount percent on the Setup sheet, then rounded the way you chose. If one product needs a special dealer price, type over the formula in that row; it will stay as you typed it.'),
               ('Does the printed list show my cost and margin?', 'No. The Price List sheet only shows code, product, category, unit, the chosen price, tax rate, price including tax and minimum order quantity.'),
               ('Can I use it without tax?', 'Yes. Leave the Tax % column at 0. The price including tax then equals the price.'),
               ('Can I show prices in two currencies?', 'Make a copy of the file for the second currency and change the currency symbol and prices there. Exchange rates move, so a separate dated list is clearer for customers.')]),

    dict(id='sales-visit-report-word', kit='sales', kind='word', icon='🚗', name='Sales Visit Report & Meeting Notes (Word)',
         title='Sales Visit Report Format in Word: Daily Field Report and Customer Meeting Notes',
         desc='Free Word templates for field sales: a daily sales visit report with calls, orders, collections and travel, and customer meeting notes covering needs, budget, decision maker and next steps.',
         files=[('Sales-Daily-Visit-Report.docx', 'Daily sales visit report (Word)'), ('Sales-Customer-Meeting-Notes.docx', 'Customer meeting notes (Word)')],
         lead='Two Word formats for salespeople who work in the field. The daily visit report tells the manager where you went, whom you met, what was ordered or collected, what competitors are doing and what you plan for tomorrow. The customer meeting notes record one important meeting in depth: the customer\'s need, budget, who decides, timeline, objections and the agreed next step, so the next call starts where this one ended.',
         inside=['Daily visit report: salesperson, date, territory, start and end reading, visit table (time, customer, contact, purpose, outcome, order value, collection, next action), day totals, competitor and market notes, expenses and tomorrow\'s plan, with manager remarks',
                 'Customer meeting notes: meeting details and attendees, current situation and need, budget, decision maker and process, timeline, competitors considered, products discussed, objections and answers, agreed next steps with owners and dates',
                 'Light shading on the parts to fill, A4 layout, prints clearly in black and white'],
         steps=['Download both files and open them in Word, Google Docs, LibreOffice or WPS Office.',
                'Type your company name in place of [Company Name] and save each as your master copy.',
                'For the daily report, open a copy each morning or evening, fill in one row of the visit table per call and total the orders and collections at the bottom.',
                'Add competitor news and customer feedback while it is fresh: these notes are often the most useful part for the manager.',
                'For an important meeting, fill in the meeting notes the same day and share them with everyone who will work on that customer.',
                'Send both as PDF (File › Save As › PDF) by email or chat, and keep a copy in a folder per month or per customer.'],
         tips=['Write outcomes, not activities: "Order for 20 cartons, delivery Friday" is more useful than "Discussed products".',
               'Every visit row should end with a next action and a date. Copy those dates into the Lead & Follow-up Tracker so they turn into reminders.',
               'If your company pays travel by distance, note the start and end readings every day; it saves arguments at month end.',
               'To add a row to a table, click in the last cell and press Tab.'],
         faqs=[('What is the difference between the two formats?', 'The visit report is a summary of a whole day with many short calls. The meeting notes go deep into one customer meeting, usually for a large or complex sale.'),
               ('What are the questions in the meeting notes based on?', 'They follow the points most sales methods ask about before a big sale: the need, the budget, who decides, the timeline and the competition. Leaving any of them blank shows what to ask in the next meeting.'),
               ('Can managers use the visit report for a weekly review?', 'Yes. Ask each salesperson to send the daily reports, or extend the visit table to cover a week and add the day in the Time column.')]),

    dict(id='sales-quotation-maker', kit='sales', kind='online', tool='sales-quote', icon='⚡', name='Online Quotation & Proforma Invoice Maker',
         title='Online Quotation Maker: Quotation or Proforma Invoice in Any Currency, Optional Tax',
         desc='Make a sales quotation or proforma invoice online: validity date, payment and delivery terms, optional GST or VAT, any currency and date format. Print, PDF, Excel or Word.',
         lead='A professional quotation or proforma invoice in a couple of minutes. Pick your currency, number style and date format once; add the customer and the items, choose whether tax is added, included or not charged, and set how many days the offer is valid. The tool works out line discounts, an overall discount, tax by rate, freight and the total in words, and shows the valid-until date and your terms. Print it, save it as PDF, or download it as Excel or Word. Your business details and settings stay on this device.',
         inside=['Quotation, proforma invoice or estimate from the same form',
                 'Any currency (rupee, dollar, euro, pound, dirham, riyal and more), number style (1,234.50, 1.234,50 or Indian 1,23,450) and date format',
                 'Valid-until date from the number of days you choose, plus payment terms, delivery terms and other conditions',
                 'Tax optional: add tax per line, prices already include tax, or no tax; tax name of your choice (GST, VAT, sales tax) with a breakdown by rate',
                 'Line discount, overall discount, freight or other charges, optional rounding and amount in words',
                 'Print / Save as PDF, download .xlsx and .docx; nothing is uploaded'],
         steps=['Type your business details at the top and choose your currency, number style, date format and tax name. They stay saved on this device.',
                'Choose Quotation, Proforma invoice or Estimate, check the number and date, and type how many days the offer is valid.',
                'Enter the customer\'s name, contact person, address and any reference such as their enquiry number.',
                'Choose how tax applies, then add the items with quantity, unit, rate, discount and tax rate.',
                'Add an overall discount or freight if needed, and edit the payment, delivery and other terms.',
                'Check the preview, then press Print / PDF or download the Excel or Word file.'],
         tips=['Keep validity short (7 to 15 days) when your own costs change often, and say so in the terms.',
               'Send a proforma invoice when the customer must pay in advance or needs a document to arrange payment; issue the proper tax invoice when you supply.',
               'Number quotations in one series (QT-001, QT-002, …) and note each one in the Lead & Follow-up Tracker with a follow-up date two or three days later.',
               'Tax rules differ by country and product. Use the rates your accountant confirms; this tool only does the arithmetic.'],
         faqs=[('What is the difference between a quotation and a proforma invoice?', 'A quotation is an offer: the price at which you are willing to supply, valid for a period. A proforma invoice is sent after the customer agrees, showing what the final invoice will be, often so they can pay in advance. Neither is a tax invoice.'),
               ('Which currencies can I use?', 'Rupee, US dollar, euro, pound, UAE dirham, Saudi riyal, Qatari riyal, Australian, Canadian, Singapore and New Zealand dollars, Nepalese, Pakistani and Sri Lankan rupees, taka, ringgit, peso, rand, naira, Kenyan shilling and yen. The amount in words uses that currency\'s name.'),
               ('What does "Prices include tax" do?', 'It treats the rates you type as tax-inclusive and works backwards to show the value before tax and the tax amount, so the total stays equal to your prices.'),
               ('Is my data saved or uploaded?', 'Nothing is uploaded. Your business details, settings and the current draft are kept only in this browser on this device; clearing site data removes them. Download or print each quotation to keep a copy.')]),
]
